My concept is to tone down the modifiers while making skills very important.

So move the roll from a d20 to a d100.

Chance that player hits monster =
Monster's AC * 5
+ Player's weapon enchantment * 5
+ players level * player's skill with weapon
+ Player's luck
+ other modifiers.  (generally *5 for the d20 to d100 shift)

So a 12th level rogue who is Expert with daggers and at maximum luck 
attempting to strike an AC -8 monster with a +2 Athane would have a base 
chance of

-8 (AC) * 5 = -40
+ 2 (athane enchantment) * 5 = -30
+ 12 (level) * 3 (Expert) = 6
+ 13 (luck) = 19
+ 2 (athane to hit bonus) * 5 = 29

Or a 29% chance to hit.

A 30th level monk who is a grandmaster striking the same monster has a 
base chance of
-8 * 5 = -40
+ 30 (level) * 5 (grandmaster) = 110
+ 13 (luck) = 123%

In order to make chances way over 100% worthwhile add a critical hit 
chance of 10% of your to-hit roll (a 2 or an 12 for these examples) and 
if you roll that then the special abiltiy of your attack (stun, behead, 
bisect, level drain past resistance, etc) is triggered.

